And underneath every branch, ask:
Was there a change?
Was there a known event?
What do the logs say?
What changed immediately before the symptom?



A compact command ladder:


DEVICE / BASELINE
-----------------
show version
show clock
show ip interface brief
show logging


PHYSICAL / INTERFACE
--------------------
show interfaces <interface>
show run interface <interface>
show controllers
show inventory


SWITCHING / L2
--------------
show interfaces status
show interfaces <port> switchport
show vlan brief
show interfaces trunk
show mac address-table
show spanning-tree
show spanning-tree vlan <vlan>
show etherchannel summary
show cdp neighbors detail
show lldp neighbors detail


L3
--
show ip arp
show ip route
show ip route <destination>
ping <destination>
ping <destination> source <source>
traceroute <destination>


VRF
---
show ip route vrf <VRF>
ping vrf <VRF> <destination> source <source>


BGP
---
show ip bgp summary
show ip bgp
show ip route <BGP-prefix>
show logging | include BGP


OSPF
----
show ip ospf neighbor
show ip ospf interface
show ip route ospf
show ip ospf database


MULTICAST
---------
show ip pim neighbor
show ip pim interface
show ip igmp groups
show ip mroute


SERIAL / T1
-----------
show interfaces serial <interface>
show controllers
show controllers t1
show logging


The important part is knowing which branch applies to the alert.

For a Se0/x/x:x down ticket, controller/serial/transport commands move near the top. For Gi1/0/20 down on an NYL access switch, VLAN/MAC/STP/PoE may matter. For Te... on a Tennessee core, you first determine whether it’s a physical interface, subinterface, PIM-facing/core link, or service interface and whether a known change explains it.







